INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('ff663b9d','Cloudflare Workflows durable execution','["Understand how steps, retries and sleep work in Workflows"]','done','max-iterations',2,2,replace('**Goal 1: Understand how steps, retries and sleep work in Workflows**\n\nThis goal was partially answered. The findings indicate that steps in Cloudflare Workflows are self-contained and individually retryable, with the ability to emit state that allows a Workflow to persist and continue from that step even if it fails due to network or infrastructure issues. To ensure durability, steps should be idempotent, meaning they can be applied multiple times without changing the result beyond the initial application. However, the findings do not provide detailed information on how retries and sleep work in conjunction with steps. \n\nThe use of idempotent steps is crucial for durable execution, as it allows a Workflow to recover from failures without compromising the integrity of the process. For example, checking if an operation is still needed before performing it can prevent unnecessary actions and ensure that the Workflow can continue from a failed step without issues. \n\nUnfortunately, no external sources (e.g. https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/) were cited in the findings to support the claims made about Cloudflare Workflows. Therefore, further research is needed to fully understand how steps, retries, and sleep work in Cloudflare Workflows. \n\nThe @cloudflare/computer package is mentioned as providing a durable filesystem and execution runtime, but its relation to steps, retries, and sleep in Workflows is not explicitly stated. \n\nIn summary, while some information is provided on how steps work in Cloudflare Workflows, the goal is only partially answered due to the lack of detailed information on retries and sleep.','\n',char(10)),1786405805912,1786405896208);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('ee08ca83','Cloudflare Vectorize semantic memory','["Understand Vectorize namespaces and metadata filtering"]','stopped','stopped',2,6,replace('1. Understand Vectorize namespaces and metadata filtering: \nAnswered. Vectorize namespaces and metadata filtering are methods to narrow the vector search space for a query. Namespaces are mutually exclusive, with a vector only being part of a single namespace, and namespace filters are applied before metadata filters. Metadata filtering supports multiple key-value pairs and various data types (string, integer, float64), requiring a metadata index for properties of these types, with specific limits (10KiB of metadata per vector, 10 metadata indexes per Vectorize index).','\n',char(10)),1786405971218,1786406053437);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('bf803830','Vectorize pricing limits','["Understand free tier vector dimension limits"]','stopped','stopped',4,5,replace('1. The free tier vector dimension limits for Vectorize are 30 million queried vector dimensions per month and 5 million stored vector dimensions, with the maximum dimensions per vector being 1536 dimensions and 32 bits precision. This goal is answered, as consistently stated across findings [1], [2], and [4]. The maximum dimensions per vector is further supported by the Limits page for account, index, and vector limits for Vectorize on Free and Paid plans, as mentioned in findings [3]. \n\nAll goals were answered.','\n',char(10)),1786406095570,1786406307166);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('caa9dd72','Vectorize pricing limits','["Understand free tier vector dimension limits"]','done','sources-exhausted',1,5,replace('1. Understand free tier vector dimension limits: \nThe free tier of Vectorize has a limit of 30 million queried vector dimensions per month and 5 million stored vector dimensions. Additional usage is billed at $0.01 per million queried vector dimensions and $0.05 per 100 million stored vector dimensions, as found in the research findings [1]. \n\nAll goals were answered.','\n',char(10)),1786406331650,1786406339772);
