INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('19ac529b','The Australian AI startup ecosystem in 2026: who the companies are, which sectors they cluster in, how they are funded, and what distinguishes AI-native companies from AI-enabled incumbents.','["Identify the main sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest","Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents, and how the ecosystem splits between them","Map the funding landscape: most active investors in Australian AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds","Identify the geographic clusters (Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth) and what differentiates them","Surface named example companies for each major sector, with sources"]','failed','Error: Too many subrequests by single Worker invocation. To configure this limit, refer to https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/configuration/#limits',11,12,NULL,1786408532605,1786410165873);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('0d5ed883','The Australian AI startup ecosystem in 2026: who the companies are, which sectors they cluster in, how they are funded, and what distinguishes AI-native companies from AI-enabled incumbents.','["Identify the main sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest","Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents, and how the ecosystem splits between them","Map the funding landscape: most active investors in Australian AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds","Identify the geographic clusters (Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth) and what differentiates them","Surface named example companies for each major sector, with sources"]','done','max-iterations',12,12,replace('**Goal 1: Identify the main sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest**\nThe main sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate include fintech, proptech, healthcare, and education, with companies like Athena, Brighte, and Airtasker operating in these areas (https://smallbizai.au/australian-ai-companies-complete-guide-by-industry-2026/). However, the findings do not provide specific information on which sectors are growing the fastest.\n\n**Goal 2: Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents, and how the ecosystem splits between them**\nAI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents based on their founding date, with AI-native companies founded in 2016 or later (https://www.airtree.vc/). The ecosystem can be split into these two categories, but the exact proportion of each is not specified in the findings.\n\n**Goal 3: Map the funding landscape: most active investors in Australian AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds**\nThe findings mention AirTree Ventures as a key venture capital firm in the Australian AI startup ecosystem (https://www.airtree.vc/). Additionally, Blackbird.vc has invested in over 130 ambitious companies, including those in the Australian AI startup ecosystem (https://www.blackbird.vc/). Notable recent rounds include Vexev''s $8.6m raise and Firmus''s $2.85 billion raise at a $15B valuation. However, the typical stages of funding are not specified.\n\n**Goal 4: Identify the geographic clusters (Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth) and what differentiates them**\nThe findings suggest that South Australia is a notable geographic cluster for AI activity in Australia, with a new partnership with OpenAI and potential for fresh research collaborations, data centre investment, and a skills makeover for the state''s public sector. However, the findings do not provide specific information on the other geographic clusters.\n\n**Goal 5: Surface named example companies for each major sector, with sources**\nNamed example companies for each major sector include:\n- Fintech: Athena, Brighte (https://smallbizai.au/australian-ai-companies-complete-guide-by-industry-2026/)\n- Proptech: Airtasker (https://smallbizai.au/australian-ai-companies-complete-guide-by-industry-2026/)\n- Healthcare: Vexev (medical robotics)\n- Education: Enrola (https://www.startmate.com/portfolio)\n- AI data centres: Firmus (https://www.startmate.com/portfolio)\n\nGoals 1, 2, 3, and 5 are partially answered, while Goal 4 is only partially answered with information on South Australia. The findings do not provide a comprehensive answer to any of the goals, and more information is needed to fully address each question.','\n',char(10)),1786410899449,1786412362708);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('2ff3e9c4','Australian AI startup ecosystem 2026','["Identify sectors and named companies","Map the funding landscape"]','stopped','test-cleanup',3,3,NULL,1786412767490,1786412800994);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('76fb1813','The Australian AI startup ecosystem in 2026: who the companies are, which sectors they cluster in, how they are funded, and what distinguishes AI-native companies from AI-enabled incumbents.','["Identify the main sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest","Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents, and how the ecosystem splits between them","Map the funding landscape: most active investors in Australian AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds","Identify the geographic clusters (Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth) and what differentiates them","Surface named example companies for each major sector, with sources"]','done','max-iterations',12,12,replace('## 1. Identify the main sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest\n**Partial**. Australian AI startups concentrate in sectors such as fintech, proptech, healthcare, and education, with examples including Athena, Archistar, and Cluey Learning (Source: [1], [3], [5], [6], [10]). However, the growth rates of these sectors are not stated in the sources.\n\n## 2. Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents, and how the ecosystem splits between them\n**Partial**. Two types of Australian AI companies are distinguished: those founded in 2016 or later, which built AI into their product from day one (e.g., Canva), and those founded before 2016, which added AI to existing products or workflows (e.g., Atlassian) (Source: [10]). However, the split between AI-native and AI-enabled companies in the ecosystem is not specified.\n\n## 3. Map the funding landscape: most active investors in Australian AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds\n**Partial**. The funding landscape includes investors like AirTree Ventures, Blackbird, and Sequoia, with notable recent funding rounds including $1.3 million in Seed funding for Aerotruth, $2 million for MagicBrief, $8.6 million for Vexev, and $2.85 billion for Firmus at a $15 billion valuation (Source: [5], [6]). Additionally, Blackbird has invested in over 130 companies, including those in sectors such as healthcare and space technologies (Source: [8]). Startmate has invested in over 300 startups with a collective portfolio value of over $4.5 billion (Source: [4], [11]).\n\n## 4. Identify the geographic clusters (Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth) and what differentiates them\n**Unanswered**. The sources do not provide specific information on the geographic clusters of Australian AI startups or what differentiates them.\n\n## 5. Surface named example companies for each major sector, with sources\n**Answered**. Examples of Australian AI startups include:\n- Fintech: Athena (Source: [1], [3], [5], [6], [10])\n- Proptech: Archistar, Landid (Source: [1], [3], [5], [6], [10], [11])\n- Healthcare: Superstat (Source: [8])\n- Education: Cluey Learning (Source: [1], [3], [5], [6], [10])\n\n## Gaps\nA follow-up run should target:\n- Growth rates of sectors where Australian AI startups concentrate\n- The split between AI-native and AI-enabled companies in the ecosystem\n- Typical funding stages for Australian AI startups\n- Differentiation of geographic clusters in Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, and Perth\n- More detailed information on the funding landscape, including investment amounts and valuations for specific companies.','\n',char(10)),1786417620890,1786418409492);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('98adcf63','The Australian and New Zealand AI startup ecosystem in 2026: who the companies are, which sectors they cluster in, how they are funded, and what distinguishes AI-native companies from AI-enabled incumbents.','["Identify the main sectors where Australian and New Zealand AI startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest","Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents, and how the ecosystem splits between them","Map the funding landscape: most active investors in Australian and New Zealand AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds","Identify the geographic clusters (Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Perth, Auckland, Wellington) and what differentiates them","Surface named example companies for each major sector, with sources, covering both Australia and New Zealand"]','failed','Error: Worker exceeded CPU time limit.',10,16,NULL,1786418661524,1786419411883);
INSERT INTO "runs" ("id","topic","goals","status","reason","iterations","max_iterations","report","created_at","updated_at") VALUES('0f4fdd5c','The New Zealand AI and technology startup ecosystem in 2026: who the companies are, which sectors they cluster in, how they are funded, and what distinguishes AI-native companies from AI-enabled incumbents.','["Identify the main sectors where New Zealand AI and technology startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest","Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents in New Zealand, and how the ecosystem splits between them","Map the funding landscape: most active investors in New Zealand AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds","Identify the geographic clusters (Auckland, Wellington, Christchurch, Dunedin) and what differentiates them","Surface named example New Zealand companies for each major sector, with sources"]','done','sources-exhausted',5,8,replace('## 1. Identify the main sectors where New Zealand AI and technology startups concentrate, and which are growing fastest\n**Unanswered**\nThe findings do not specify the main sectors where New Zealand AI and technology startups concentrate, or which are growing fastest. \n\n## 2. Establish how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents in New Zealand, and how the ecosystem splits between them\n**Unanswered**\nThe findings do not provide information on how AI-native companies are distinguished from AI-enabled incumbents in New Zealand, or how the ecosystem splits between them.\n\n## 3. Map the funding landscape: most active investors in New Zealand AI, typical stages, and notable recent rounds\n**Partial**\nThe New Zealand government has a $300m fund of funds, Elevate, which invests into venture capital funds to fill the Series A and B capital gap for high-growth New Zealand tech companies (https://www.callaghaninnovation.govt.nz/). The NZGCP also has an Aspire fund that invests directly into early-stage Kiwi technology start-ups at proof of concept, seed, and early expansion investment stages. However, the findings do not provide information on the most active investors in New Zealand AI or notable recent rounds.\n\n## 4. Identify the geographic clusters (Auckland, Wellington, Christchurch, Dunedin) and what differentiates them\n**Partial**\nWellington is home to Creative HQ, a leading innovation hub that supports startups, founders, and innovators in New Zealand (https://www.creativehq.co.nz/). Auckland is the largest city in New Zealand, but the findings do not provide further information on the geographic clusters or what differentiates them.\n\n## 5. Surface named example New Zealand companies for each major sector, with sources\n**Unanswered**\nThe findings mention Biostart and Dot Ingredients as companies that have worked with Callaghan Innovation, but do not provide named example New Zealand companies for each major sector.\n\n## Gaps\nA follow-up run should target the identification of main sectors where New Zealand AI and technology startups concentrate, the distinction between AI-native companies and AI-enabled incumbents, the most active investors in New Zealand AI, notable recent funding rounds, and a more detailed analysis of geographic clusters. Additionally, surfacing named example New Zealand companies for each major sector would provide valuable insights into the ecosystem.','\n',char(10)),1786420571980,1786420878190);
